[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What makes a task one a user "cares about"? → Implicit + overrides (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: where notifications actually show up. This one drives how much infrastructure the design carries.

  - [1mInbox only[0m — a bell with an unread count opening a list of notifications, persisted with read/unread state. This is
    the durable core; nothing is missed because you weren't looking.
  - [1mToasts only[0m — transient pop-ups when something changes. Lively, but there's no history, so anything you miss is
    gone. Weak on its own.
  - [1mInbox + toasts[0m — inbox is the record, toasts surface things arriving while you're on the page. Toasts are a thin
    read of the same store, so the marginal cost over inbox-only is small.
  - [1mPlus browser/OS notifications[0m — the Notification API adds a permission prompt and, for anything meaningful, a
    service worker. In a no-backend app it can only fire while the tab is open, which is exactly when a toast already
    works.

  I recommend inbox + toasts, and explicitly leaving OS notifications out. The first three all read from one store, so
  toasts cost little; OS notifications add real machinery (permissions, service worker) to do something the toast
  already does in the only situation this app can support.

  Worth noting: because identity is a local picker, you can switch users in one browser. I'd have the store sync across
  tabs (BroadcastChannel), which makes "another user changed your task" actually demonstrable — and is the same shape a
  server push would take later.
